# Template. Copy to .env and fill in the values.
# The .env file itself never reaches git — see .gitignore.

# Connection string for the Supabase database.
#
# Where to get it: the "Connect" button at the top of the project dashboard (not
# under Settings — the dashboard moved it). The dialog offers several methods:
# a direct connection, and shared pooler entries in session and transaction mode.
#
# Take the SESSION POOLER, and only it. The other two both fail, for different
# reasons:
#
#   - the TRANSACTION pooler (port 6543) does not hold a session the way pg_dump
#     requires;
#   - the DIRECT connection ("db.<project-ref>.supabase.co") exists only over IPv6.
#     Verified 28.08: that host has an AAAA record and no A record at all, and the
#     Docker container has no IPv6 route, so pg_dump dies with "Network
#     unreachable" before any password is ever checked.
#
# The session pooler host answers over IPv4. Recognise it by the word "pooler" in
# the host name — not by the port, which is 5432 for the direct connection too.
#
# Format: postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres
#
# Two things that trip people up:
#   - the user is not a bare "postgres" but carries the project identifier,
#     "postgres.<project-ref>". That is normal — copy the string whole;
#   - the dashboard prints the string with a "[YOUR-PASSWORD]" placeholder. Replace
#     it, square brackets included, with the real database password.
#
# This is NOT the same thing as VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY from the application. That
# key is public by design, goes to the browser and speaks to the REST API under RLS.
# pg_dump connects straight to Postgres and needs real database credentials.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=
